
> P-spline Regression Example

P-spline (penalized B-spline) regression on the Doppler function: noisy samples, the true mean, an unpenalized B-spline fit (`BSR`, green) and a P-spline fit (`PSR`, blue) on the same 100 evenly spaced knots -- the same program in C++ and in each language binding: C#, D, Go, Guile, Java, JavaScript, Lua, OCaml, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, R, Ruby and Tcl.

Both take the knot vector directly: `BSR(x, y, knots)` and `PSR(x, y, knots, lam)` fit cubic B-splines with breakpoints at `knots` (here `seql(0, 1, nk)`; an integer basis count in place of `knots` gives evenly spaced knots too). `PSR` (`PSplineRegression`) minimizes \( \|y - B\beta\|^2 + \lambda \|D_2 \beta\|^2 \), a second-order difference penalty on neighbouring coefficients. With a rich basis the penalty, not the knot count, controls smoothness: `BSR` on 100 knots chases the noise, while `PSR` stays smooth where the signal is slow and still tracks the fast oscillations near zero. Here \( \lambda \) is set by `lam` next to `nk`: larger values give a smoother fit (fewer effective degrees of freedom), and \( \lambda \to 0 \) approaches the unpenalized `BSR` fit. `PSR(x, y, knots, lam)` also takes the spline degree and penalty order as optional trailing arguments. Leaving out \( \lambda \) entirely, as in `PSR(x, y, knots)`, picks it by generalized cross-validation, \( \mathrm{GCV}(\lambda) = n \, \mathrm{RSS} / (n - \mathrm{edf})^2 \).

\tabs(
\tab(C++,
```{cpp, results="output", exports="both", flags="-std=c++17 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <iostream>
#include <datamunge/datamunge.hpp>

namespace dm = datamunge;

dm::Vector doppler(dm::Vector x) {
  return dm::sin(20/(x + 0.25));
}

int main(void) {
  int n = 5000;
  int nk = 100; // Number of knots
  double lam = 1.0; // Smoothing parameter: larger is smoother
  dm::Vector x = dm::unif(0, 1, n);
  dm::Vector y = doppler(x) + dm::rnorm(0, 1, n); // y = f(x) + epsilon
  dm::Vector xseq = dm::seql(0, 1, n);
  dm::Vector knots = dm::seql(0, 1, nk);
  dm::Vector yseq = doppler(xseq);
  dm::BSR bsr = dm::BSR(x, y, knots);
  dm::PSR psr = dm::PSR(x, y, knots, lam);
  dm::Vector bfit = bsr.predict(xseq);
  dm::Vector pfit = psr.predict(xseq);
  dm::Plot plot = dm::Plot()
    .points(x, y, "samples", dm::RGBA{237, 199, 135, 0.6}, 6.0)
    .lines(xseq, yseq, "True Mean", dm::RGB{200, 10, 44}, 6.0)
    .lines(xseq, bfit, "B-spline fit (BSR)", dm::RGB{46, 160, 67}, 6.0)
    .lines(xseq, pfit, "P-spline fit (PSR)", dm::RGB{44, 10, 200}, 6.0)
    .title("Doppler data: B-spline vs P-spline")
    .xlab("X")
    .ylab("doppler(X)");
  std::cout <<  plot.to_svg() << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```
